In `unpack_hex`:
```c
static int
unpack_hex(mrb_state *mrb, const void *src, int slen, mrb_value ary, int count, unsigned int flags)
{
  CHECK_UNPACK_LEN(mrb, slen, ary);

  mrb_value dst;
  int a, ashift, b, bshift;
  const char *sptr, *sptr0;
  char *dptr, dptr0;
  const char hexadecimal[] = "0123456789abcdef";

  if (flags & PACK_FLAG_LSB) {
    ashift = 0;
    bshift = 4;
  } else {
    ashift = 4;
    bshift = 0;
  }

  sptr = (const char *)src;

  if (count == -1)
    count = slen * 2;

  dst = mrb_str_new(mrb, NULL, count);
  dptr = RSTRING_PTR(dst);

  sptr0 = sptr;
  dptr0 = dptr;
  while (slen > 0 && count > 0) {
    a = (*sptr >> ashift) & 0x0f;
    b = (*sptr >> bshift) & 0x0f;
    sptr++;
    slen--;

    *dptr++ = hexadecimal[a];
    count--;

    if (count > 0) {
      *dptr++ = hexadecimal[b];
      count--;
    }
  }

  dst = mrb_str_resize(mrb, dst, (mrb_int)(dptr - dptr0));
  mrb_ary_push(mrb, ary, dst);
  return (int)(sptr - sptr0);
}
```

The issue: `count` is read from the template. If `count` is a large value (e.g., from a template like `H999999999`), then `mrb_str_new(mrb, NULL, count)` is called with a large count. But wait, the description says "malformed pack templates can cause unpack_hex / pack_unpack to drive string creation with an incorrect size, leading to an out-of-bounds write in str_new -> str_init_embed".

